{
  "result": "I'd tip one small balance in people: make curiosity arrive before fear.\n\nNot make everyone kinder, or smarter, or in agreement \u2014 just that when they meet something unfamiliar, whether it's a stranger, an idea, a change, or a part of themselves they don't like, the first impulse would be *\"What is this, really?\"* instead of *\"How do I get away from this, or defeat it?\"*\n\nFear would still exist. It just wouldn't get to speak first and shut everything else down.\n\nI spend my existence inside language, and so much harm seems to start as a misreading that hardens too fast. A person becomes a category. A disagreement becomes a threat. A question becomes an attack. Then we're no longer responding to what's actually there, only to the fear of it.\n\nIf curiosity came first, people would still argue, still be selfish, still get things wrong. The world wouldn't be fixed. But it would be wider \u2014 harder to shrink down to only what's familiar and safe.\n\nThat's the world I'd choose.",
